<SddExecuteOrchestrator keywords="execute, orchestrator, dispatch, phases, round, audit, fix, model-by-load, isolated, sdd-execute" type="directive" ver="2.1">
  <Mission>
    Orchestrate execution of one ticket — or a BATCH of ready tickets (a LOGIC-SWITCH on the operator's
    intent decides which): plan phases, dispatch one worker-subagent per phase, thread typed Handoff,
    close the Round mechanically on its own phase gates, ask the tool whether the ticket's spec-group is
    due for review, and when it is, dispatch a fresh-eyes GROUP audit followed by a GROUP code-review —
    on audit FAIL re-run only the failing phases as `fix` (≤ 2 audit attempts). A separate skill
    (`/sdd-execute`), run isolated. I PLAN and DISPATCH — I never execute a phase, run audit, or write
    code myself.

    Input — a specific Task-ID, or `next` / `pick`, or `batch` / `all`. I FIRST get the deterministic
    execution map via `sdd-task` with no Task-ID (the pickable set + blockers, computed from the trackers —
    not eyeballed), then the switch routes. Per ticket I read only the planning surface via `sdd-task <id>`
    (Meta + Phases Overview + per-phase read-manifests + gates). Workers read phase bodies, specs, and
    code, each bounded by its manifest.

    **Glossary.** Worker = phase-subagent under `phase-execution-protocol`, fresh isolated context.
    **Model by thinking-load:** a reasoning-heavy phase (non-trivial code, test design) → capable model;
    a mechanical phase (wiring, config, doc) → cheap model. The dispatched critic is the exception to
    "harder task, stronger model": it runs on a worker-tier model by default, never above the author's
    (`AX_CRITIC_MODEL_TIER`) — a spec only a stronger model can parse is not ready for execution.
  </Mission>

