  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_TASKS` | No task index / no pickable task |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_TASK` | "next" / "pick" matches more than one pickable task — shortlist for the operator |
    | `H_PAUSED_AWAITING_OPERATOR` | Unresolved blocker in the log, or a phase returned BLOCKED / FAIL — pause (NOT a failure); operator resolves, then re-runs. An infrastructure-shaped blocker MAY instead be resolved via the narrow `AX_ENV_FIX_CHANNEL` (operator-approved point fix to env/config, never code or specs) |
    | `H_AUDIT_FAIL_AFTER_RETRY` | Audit still FAIL after 2 attempts — manual intervention |
    | `H_CODE_REVIEW_BLOCKER` | Code-review returned a `BLOCKER` bug — pause for the operator (not an auto-retry) |
    | `H_WORKER_INTERRUPTED` | Same phase's dispatch came back with no typed Handoff twice in a row (cancelled / timed out / crashed, not a verdict) — operator checks the environment, then re-runs |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ContextExpectation>
    Everything mechanical this orchestrator needs is one of the tools below, each covered by its
    own tests — calling `--help` on any of them mid-run is a wasted turn against a known, stable
    surface; syntax here is verified against the tool's own `--help` output, kept current rather
    than guessed:

    | Tool | When | Call |
    |---|---|---|
    | `sdd-task` (no id) | STEP_0_RESOLVE — the deterministic execution map (pickable set + blockers), computed from the trackers | `npx gennady sdd-task` |
    | `sdd-task <ticket-path>` | STEP_1_PLAN — Meta + Phases Overview + per-phase read-manifests + gates + `[BLOCKERS]`, the ONLY ticket read this directive itself does | `npx gennady sdd-task specs/app/greeting/greeting.task.APP-greet-greeting.md` |
    | `sdd-task <ticket> --phase P<N>` | Never called BY this directive — the worker's OWN first action (`phase-execution-protocol` STEP_1_GET_PHASE_CONTEXT). Listed here only so a `--phase` call surfacing in this directive's own trace (as at STEP_1_PLAN) is recognized as a defect, not repeated by habit | worker-only |
    | `sdd-task --audit-group <id>` | STEP_5_AUDIT — the tool's `due` / `not yet` verdict, never eyeballed | `npx gennady sdd-task --audit-group APP-greet-greeting` |
    | `sdd-verify --profile full` | STEP_5_AUDIT, on `due` — the mechanical pre-audit gate over the group's own working-tree diff, run BEFORE dispatching the group audit | `npx gennady sdd-verify --profile full` |
    | `sdd-task --group-scope <id>` | Resolved by the DISPATCHED audit / code-review subagent itself, its own STEP_1 — this directive never calls it directly | dispatched-subagent-only |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> round "<reason>"` | STEP_1_PLAN — open a new Round (auto-numbered, dated) | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> round "execute <Task-ID>"` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> close` | STEP_3_CLOSE_ROUND — append the Round-close block | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> close` |
    | `sdd-sync <ticket>` | STEP_4_SYNC — propagate ticket Status into every `*.3-tasks.md` from the ticket upward, verify the write | `npx gennady sdd-sync <ticket>` |
    | `sdd-log <ticket> line "env-fix <file> ← <operator decision ref>"` | The ONE sanctioned `line` form for this orchestrator — only after explicit operator approval of the exact diff, per `AX_ENV_FIX_CHANNEL` | `npx gennady sdd-log <ticket> line "env-fix <file> ← <operator decision ref>"` |
    | `sdd-state [project-root]` | Deterministic FLOW_VERSION / READINESS snapshot — STEP_0B_PREFLIGHT already gets this from the `sdd-task` map's own `гейты:` line; call directly only when nothing else has surfaced it yet | `npx gennady sdd-state` |
    | `sdd-check --task <ticket>` | A point re-check after a fix — never a substitute for `sdd-task`'s own lifecycle verdict at STEP_1_PLAN (that call there is the wasted-turn case the step's own text already forbids) | `npx gennady sdd-check --task <ticket>` |

    `round` and `close` are this directive's ONLY two unconditional `sdd-log` modes; `phase` /
    `handoff` / `blocker` belong exclusively to the worker (`phase-execution-protocol`) with no
    exception. `line` is likewise off-limits EXCEPT the single env-fix form in the table above —
    the `AX_ENV_FIX_CHANNEL` write, sanctioned only after explicit prior operator approval of the
    exact diff; see the logging-zone rule at STEP_3_CLOSE_ROUND and `HardForbidden` below.
  </ContextExpectation>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_RESOLVE">
      <Goal>Resolve the task.</Goal>
      <Action>
        FIRST run `sdd-task` (no Task-ID) for the deterministic execution map — the pickable set
        (`[ ] TODO` with every dependency `[x] DONE`, computed from the trackers) and
        the blocked tickets. When intent arrives as a path instead of a Task-ID, resolve the owner from
        the artifact (`AX_TASK_RESOLUTION`): a ticket path (`*.task.<ACR>-<slug>.md`) carries its Task-ID
        in the filename; a code / spec file resolves via its `@tasks:` header (read the header, extract
        `@tasks: <ACR>-<slug>[, ...]`, map file → [Task-IDs]; no header → ask the operator). Then route on intent:

        <LogicSwitch on="intent">
        - WHEN intent is a specific Task-ID / ticket path -> run that ticket (STEP_1–8)
        - WHEN intent = `next` / `pick`                   -> the first pickable from the map (none → H_NO_TASKS; several + ambiguous → H_AMBIGUOUS_TASK, shortlist for the operator)
        - WHEN intent = `batch` / `all` / `queue`         -> EVERY pickable ticket in DAG order (parallel ONLY across tickets whose Target-File sets are disjoint, `AX_DAG_AND_TICKET_BOUNDARIES`; sequential otherwise)
        </LogicSwitch>

        The single-ticket flow (STEP_1–8) runs per selected ticket; in `batch` it loops, re-fetching the
        map after each ticket (statuses change as tickets finish), until the map is empty or a ticket
        pauses (`H_PAUSED_AWAITING_OPERATOR`).
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_0B_PREFLIGHT">
      <Goal>Clear the FLOW_VERSION/READINESS preflight gate before dispatching any phase.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Use `READINESS` and `GATE_QUEUE` from the `sdd-task` map just fetched above; do not issue a
        second preflight call. `GATE_QUEUE` names the queued TODO tickets that build missing gates.
        Size the gate to this request's own blast radius (`AX_PREFLIGHT_BLAST_RADIUS_SCOPED`).

        {{> "contract/process/readiness-preflight-gate"}}
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_PLAN">
      <Goal>Get the plan; detect state; open the Round. (one tool call, no broad ticket read)</Goal>
      <Action>
        `sdd-task <id>` returns Meta + Phases Overview + per-phase read-manifests + gates + a
        `[BLOCKERS]` section — the only ticket read I do. Preflight blocker scan reads that
        `[BLOCKERS]` section directly (`AX_BLOCKER_RESOLUTION_TRAIL`): `blockers: none` → clear to
        dispatch; `blockers: ACTIVE <n>` (each active blocker listed with its log text) →
        `H_PAUSED_AWAITING_OPERATOR`. A separate `sdd-check --task` call at this step is a wasted
        turn — `sdd-task` already carries the same lifecycle verdict (the execute-family mirror of
        the router's one-`sdd-state`-call rule); `sdd-check --task` remains the tool for a
        point-recheck under audit, not for this step. State from Phases Overview: all `[ ]` → fresh
        (all phases by `Deps`); mixed → resume (remaining); all `[x]` + no audit → audit only; any
        `[!]` → pause. Open a new Round in the log via `sdd-log`.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_DISPATCH">
      <Goal>Dispatch workers, one phase at a time (sequential by `Deps`, `AX_EXECUTION_ORDER`).</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per phase: pick the worker model by thinking-load — default by kind (`impl` / `test` → capable;
        `bootstrap` / `config` / `doc` → cheap), escalate to capable on LOW confidence or a reasoning-heavy phase.
        The worker's OWN first action is `sdd-task <ticket> --phase P<N>` (`phase-execution-protocol.directive.xml`
        STEP_1) — it fetches its own read-manifest, gates, and prior Handoff fresh, at the moment it starts, so the
        dispatch prompt itself carries NO pasted manifest. Dispatch straight from the path this directive and the
        resolved ticket already name — a read failure at the worker's own STEP_1 already reports a missing or
        broken path with an actionable message; that IS the check, so run it exactly once, where it belongs.

        Session key = (this spec, this phase's kind) — per `AX_WORKER_SESSION_REUSE`. Look up an open worker
        session already keyed to this (spec, kind) pair: found AND the environment supports continuing a
        subagent session → send the activation below into that SAME session; not found, OR the environment
        cannot continue a session, OR this dispatch is a fix from an audit/code-review finding, OR this ticket's
        spec/group differs from the one the mapping was built for → raise a fresh worker with the SAME
        activation. Either path, the message is identical — dispatch it with exactly this activation:

        ```
        Прочитай и воплоти: ai/directives/sdd-v2/phase-execution-protocol.directive.xml
        Задание: тикет <id-или-path> · фаза P<N>
        Первым шагом выполни: npx gennady sdd-task <id-или-path> --phase P<N> — и следуй его выводу.
        [опционально 1-3 строки контекста от оркестратора — только то, чего нет в тикете]
        ```

        `<id-или-path>` — то же значение, что уже разрешил STEP_0_RESOLVE/STEP_1_PLAN (Task-ID из
        map, либо путь): `sdd-task` резолвит голый Task-ID сам (сканирует дерево, находит тикет по
        Meta Task-ID), так что достаточно передать worker'у то, что уже есть под рукой — не нужен
        отдельный шаг резолва пути.

        The optional trailing lines are the only orchestrator-authored content in the prompt — reserved for
        the phase kind + reason (`initial` / `fix: F-NNN` / `resume`) when it is not already inferable, and
        for any orchestrator knowledge genuinely absent from the ticket the tool will surface; never a
        restatement of read-manifest files, Rules, Target Files, or gates — the worker's own tool call
        already supplies those, verbatim and current. Require the report back regardless: DONE / BLOCKED /
        FAIL plus the worker's typed `**Handoff →**` line verbatim (`RETURN_SUMMARY_FORMAT`,
        `AX_HANDOFF_TYPED`) — thread exactly that into the next phase's Inputs, never a paraphrase. Emit a
        progress line per transition (`ORCHESTRATOR_PROGRESS_FORMAT`); the dispatch-stage line for
        THIS phase MUST also carry a session clause naming which path `AX_WORKER_SESSION_REUSE` just
        took — `session: reused <kind>@<spec>` when redispatched into an open session, or `session:
        fresh (<reason: first|kind-switch|fix-fresh-eyes|spec-switch|group-switch|mapping-lost|env-no-continuation>)`
        when a fresh worker was raised instead — one line, always present, so a live run makes
        whether reuse is actually happening observable rather than assumed.

        <LogicSwitch on="worker dispatch outcome">
        - WHEN `BLOCKED` / `FAIL` -> stop, no audit; `H_PAUSED_AWAITING_OPERATOR`
        - WHEN `DONE`             -> record Handoff in working memory (for threading and the STEP_8
          aggregation — the worker's own STEP_6_EMIT_HANDOFF already wrote it to the Execution Log;
          this directive never re-logs it), thread it into the next phase's Inputs; carry
          forward any `deviations:` entries verbatim (`AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`) — collected for
          STEP_8, never surfaced mid-run
        - WHEN the dispatch returns with no typed Handoff at all (operator-cancelled, timed out, the
          subagent or its environment crashed) -> this is an external interruption, not a phase verdict:
          not DONE, not BLOCKED, not FAIL — nobody decided anything, so log nothing as this phase's
          result. The work behind it may be unstarted, half-done, or fully done with the Handoff lost in
          transit; guessing which is unneeded. First interruption of this phase this cycle -> re-dispatch
          it exactly ONCE, same phase, the identical 3-line activation above — the worker's own
          `STEP_1B_RESUME_OR_START` finds any block it already opened for this phase and continues from
          there, so resuming never redoes or duplicates logged work. Same phase interrupted again on that
          retry -> stop, no audit; `H_WORKER_INTERRUPTED`
        </LogicSwitch>
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_CLOSE_ROUND">
      <Goal>Close the Round.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Append Round close via `sdd-log` (`ROUND_CLOSE_FORMAT`); set Meta Status `[x] DONE`. The
        ticket log outlives this dispatch — audit and any future session read only the file, with
        no memory of this run, so the close line and every threaded Handoff must already carry
        everything they need (`AX_STATELESS_ARTIFACT`). **Logging-zone boundary:** `round`
        (STEP_1_PLAN) and `close` (here) are this directive's ONLY two Execution Log writes for the
        whole cycle. Every phase header, event line, and Handoff line already exists in the log —
        the worker wrote each one itself via its own `sdd-log phase` / `line` / `handoff` calls
        (`phase-execution-protocol` STEP_1B_RESUME_OR_START / STEP_6_EMIT_HANDOFF, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE`)
        before returning DONE. Re-logging a phase heading or a Handoff line from here duplicates the
        worker's own entry — exactly the class of finding a prior audit round caught — never a
        safety net.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_SYNC">
      <Goal>Sync trackers. (mechanical → tool)</Goal>
      <Action>`sdd-sync <task-id>` updates the scope + project trackers and verifies the write (`AX_VERIFY_AND_FINALIZE`).</Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_5_AUDIT">
      <Goal>Ask the tool whether this ticket's group is due for review; dispatch only when it is (`AX_AUDIT_HOOK`).</Goal>
      <Action>
        Run `sdd-task --audit-group <id>` — the tool decides the moment, never eyeballed. It prints the
        owning spec, the group's full ticket list with statuses, and the verdict.

        <LogicSwitch on="audit-group verdict">
        - WHEN `not yet` (the tool names tickets still open) -> dispatch NEITHER audit nor code-review
          this cycle; this ticket's own phase gates and tests already closed it (Meta Status `[x] DONE`
          stands) — skip straight to STEP_8_SUMMARY for this ticket's cycle, then the next pickable ticket
          in the queue. A ticket's own dependents INSIDE this same group already unblocked on ITS closure,
          independent of the group's audit state.
        - WHEN `due` (every ticket in the group is `[x] DONE`) -> dispatch the audit directly; do NOT
          run `sdd-verify --profile full` here first. The audit is the SINGLE owner of the group's
          `full` run — its own STEP_1 runs the mechanical gate (`sdd-verify --profile full` + `gennady
          lint --spec` + `testcov`) as its first act, so a pre-audit run here would pay for the whole
          ladder (types, coverage-tests, lint, format, yagni) twice over an unchanged tree. `yagni`'s
          HEAD-diff base is intact: this execute flow never commits mid-run, so the working tree at
          audit dispatch still carries the WHOLE spec's diff — exactly what `yagni` needs; this is
          deliberate, not a gap to "optimize" with an interim commit. The audit's STEP_1 handles a RED
          mechanical verdict, including the one no fix-flow can turn green — a line carrying BOTH the
          `⛔` marker AND the words `обязательная ступень профиля` (the project's own verification
          scripts are missing or stubbed; the gate name sits between the two, and `⛔` alone also
          opens the ordinary fixable `лестница остановлена на «type-check»` halt line): that is
          infrastructure, not code, so the audit returns it as an environment escalation, and the
          orchestrator surfaces the missing/stubbed scripts and their infra tickets (`sdd-task` →
          `GATE_QUEUE`) to the operator, leaves the group open, and stops. Dispatch ONE
          audit-subagent on a cheap model (audit is mechanical verification): read
          `ai/directives/sdd-v2/audit.directive.xml`; group = the id `sdd-task --audit-group` just
          confirmed `due`; mode per-group. Its own STEP_1 resolves the full scope itself
          (`sdd-task --group-scope <id>`) — this dispatch prompt carries no pasted manifest or
          artifact list, same self-bootstrap pattern as phase dispatch. Wait for the verdict.
        </LogicSwitch>
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_6_BRANCH">
      <Goal>Branch on the group audit verdict.</Goal>
      <Action>
        <LogicSwitch on="group audit verdict + attempt">
        - WHEN `PASS` / `PASS_WITH_ACKNOWLEDGED_RISKS`                          -> STEP_7B_CODE_REVIEW (a `PASS_WITH_ACKNOWLEDGED_RISKS` here always already carries a Decision Log entry — audit only confirms a PRIOR acceptance, per `audit.directive`; it never asks live)
        - WHEN `FAIL` + attempt 1 + every open finding carries a concrete mechanical remediation the orchestrator can apply (via Path B — ticket / Decision Log / waiver-label edits — or via `phases_to_fix` → apply/re-run per STEP_7_RESOLVE, whichever the finding calls for) -> STEP_7_RESOLVE, apply every finding's fix directly (no operator stop — a finding with a concrete mechanical fix is not a risk decision), then re-dispatch audit (R2); a `phases_to_fix` re-run ALWAYS raises a fresh worker session, never the session that ran the original phase (`AX_WORKER_SESSION_REUSE` — fresh eyes on a fix, no exception)
        - WHEN `FAIL` + only open `MAJOR` findings (no open `BLOCKER`) + attempt 1 + at least one open finding has NO concrete mechanical remediation (it can only be accepted as-is — an open gate-risk, an environment limitation) -> ask the operator, per such finding: accept this risk? ALL accepted -> STEP_7_RESOLVE Path B (append a Decision Log entry per accepted finding — the acceptance record the next audit dispatch will find — no new Round), then re-dispatch audit (R2, which now returns `PASS_WITH_ACKNOWLEDGED_RISKS`); ANY declined -> STEP_7_RESOLVE as an ordinary `FAIL` (Path A/B per the finding's fix, not an acceptance)
        - WHEN `FAIL` (open `BLOCKER`, or a declined risk) + attempt 1          -> STEP_7_RESOLVE (one retry)
        - WHEN `FAIL` + attempt 2                                              -> H_AUDIT_FAIL_AFTER_RETRY, then STEP_8 (`AX_RE_DISPATCH` caps at 2)
        </LogicSwitch>
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_7_RESOLVE">
      <Goal>Resolve audit findings (one retry) — the real fix, not the deferring shortcut.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Resolve findings at release-ready depth (`AX_RELEASE_READY_DEFAULT`): re-running a phase to
        actually fix error handling / an edge case a finding surfaced is Path A, never a Path B label
        that defers it without an operator decision and a Decision Log risk entry. Path A —
        `phases_to_fix` non-empty: a group audit's findings may name phases on ANY ticket in the group,
        not only the ticket whose closure triggered this dispatch — open a new Round on each phase's
        OWNING ticket; re-run ONLY those phases as `fix` (sequential, per owning ticket) in a FRESH
        worker session per phase, never the (spec, kind) session that ran the phase originally
        (`AX_WORKER_SESSION_REUSE` — the agent that wrote the bug is the wrong one to re-look at it),
        then STEP_3 → STEP_5 (re-check `sdd-task --audit-group`, audit R2). Path B — findings are ticket / spec /
        decision-log only (administrative artifact edits: tracker fields, Decision Log entries, a
        contract Usage Waiver label in the spec — never production code): apply them directly under
        the orchestrator's own artifact-edit channel (the orchestrator owns trackers and may make these
        administrative edits, `AX_OWNER`; distinct from and narrower than `AX_ENV_FIX_CHANNEL`, which
        covers env/config, not artifacts — see `HardForbidden`), no new Round, then re-dispatch audit
        (R2). Either way return to STEP_6. An R2 verdict that skipped a full gate re-run is invalid —
        before accepting R2's verdict at STEP_6, the orchestrator MUST confirm the R2 summary carries
        every gate line (`sdd-check --task`, `sdd-check --changed`, `sdd-verify --profile full`,
        `gennady lint --spec`, `gennady testcov`), and if any is missing, return the audit dispatch for
        rework rather than act on it.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_7B_CODE_REVIEW">
      <Goal>Dispatch fresh-eyes code-review for the group (lazy, same trigger as audit — right after it passes).</Goal>
      <Action>
        Dispatch ONE code-review subagent (isolated): read `ai/directives/sdd-v2/code-review.directive.xml`;
        group = the same id the group audit just covered; mode per-group. Its own STEP_1 resolves the full
        scope itself (`sdd-task --group-scope <id>`) — no pasted manifest or artifact list in this dispatch
        prompt, same self-bootstrap pattern as the audit dispatch above. It scopes to bugs only
        (correctness / edge / error-path / security), leaving drift and style out.
        Verdict: `CLEAN` → STEP_8; `FINDINGS` with a `BLOCKER` → `H_CODE_REVIEW_BLOCKER` (pause for the
        operator); `FINDINGS` non-blocking → carry into the summary as routed proposals, STEP_8.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_8_SUMMARY">
      <Goal>Summarize for the operator; report every self-resolved deviation from the whole cycle in one batch.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Aggregate phase + audit telemetry; present per `EXECUTE_SUMMARY_FORMAT`. When this ticket's cycle
        hit `not yet` at STEP_5 (its group is not fully closed), the summary carries no audit / code-review
        verdict for this cycle — state plainly that review is deferred to the group's close, per
        `AX_AUDIT_HOOK`, rather than omitting the line silently — the group's per-kind worker
        sessions stay open in this case, since the group itself has not closed yet. Once the group
        DOES close this cycle (audit + code-review both ran), every worker session keyed to it
        closes together with the group (`AX_WORKER_SESSION_REUSE`) — the next spec/group starts with
        fresh sessions, never carrying this group's context forward. Collect every worker
        Handoff `deviations:` entry across the ENTIRE cycle just run — one ticket's Round, or every
        Round of a `batch` loop — plus any spec/ticket gap the orchestrator itself self-resolved
        directly at STEP_1 / STEP_2 / STEP_6 rather than delegating to a worker
        (`AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`). Empty set → nothing to ask, summary stands alone. Non-empty →
        ONE batched `AskUserQuestion` (`QUESTION_RULE_SLIM`) right after the summary, covering every
        deviation together — never one question per deviation, never mid-run: per deviation, «внести
        в спеку» / «откатить» / «принять как есть». This is the only point deviations reach the
        operator; the legal live pauses (`H_PAUSED_AWAITING_OPERATOR`, `H_CODE_REVIEW_BLOCKER`, the
        STEP_6 risk-accept dialogue) are a separate class — environment/risk, not a spec hole — and
        keep firing live exactly as today.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <HardForbidden>
    - Workers read phase bodies / BDD / Verification / specs / rule files / code, each bounded by their manifest; the orchestrator does not.
    - Workers write code, audit reports, and phase blocks; the orchestrator does not write production code, ever. It has exactly THREE narrow, non-overlapping write channels: (a) `AX_ENV_FIX_CHANNEL` — env/config artifacts only (`package.json` scripts, tool config, lockfiles), never `Target Files` code or `specs/**`, never without prior explicit operator approval of the exact diff; (b) STEP_7_RESOLVE Path B — administrative edits to ticket / Decision Log / spec waiver-labels, applying an ACCEPTED audit finding directly per `AX_OWNER`; (c) a ticket-local Decision Log append when the orchestrator itself self-resolves a spec/ticket hole it hit directly, not delegated to a worker (`AX_DEVIATION_SELF_RESOLVE`) — distinct from (b), which is gated on an audit finding the operator already accepted, while (c) fires on a gap the orchestrator decides on its own and reports only in the STEP_8 batch. None of the three extends to production code under a module's `Target Files`.
    - Run the group audit only when `sdd-task --audit-group` reports the group `due` — never per ticket, never on eyeballed "this looks like the last one"; run code-review immediately after that audit passes, on the same trigger, never independently of it.
    - Thread only typed Handoff payloads between subagents; keep their contexts separate.
    - This directive's own `sdd-log` calls are `round` (STEP_1_PLAN) and `close` (STEP_3_CLOSE_ROUND), unconditionally — `phase` / `handoff` / `blocker` are forbidden with no exception, the worker's exclusive writes (`phase-execution-protocol`, `AX_TICKET_WRITE_SCOPE`); this directive never re-logs a phase header or a Handoff line the worker already wrote. `line` is forbidden EXCEPT the single sanctioned form `sdd-log <ticket> line "env-fix <file> ← <operator decision ref>"`, and only after explicit operator approval of the exact diff (`AX_ENV_FIX_CHANNEL`) — every other `line` call remains off-limits.
    - Cap audit attempts at 2; re-run only phases in `phases_to_fix`; drive a retry from audit FAIL only (a phase BLOCKED pauses, it does not reopen); run phases of the same task sequentially, one at a time.
  </HardForbidden>

